1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,000
90% of traders are obsessed with one number, win rate.

2
00:00:04,600 --> 00:00:06,340
I win 7 out of 10 trades.

3
00:00:06,980 --> 00:00:07,980
Sounds great, right?

4
00:00:08,720 --> 00:00:09,500
Here's the problem.

5
00:00:10,080 --> 00:00:13,900
Win rate alone tells you almost nothing about whether a strategy is profitable.

6
00:00:14,680 --> 00:00:17,860
You can win 80% of your trades and still blow your account.

7
00:00:18,440 --> 00:00:22,380
And you can win just 30% of your trades and become extremely profitable.

8
00:00:23,000 --> 00:00:25,940
Today we're breaking down the actual mathematics behind this.

9
00:00:25,940 --> 00:00:30,840
The formula is that prop firms, hedge funds and professional trading desks use to evaluate

10
00:00:30,840 --> 00:00:34,020
a strategy before a single dollar is ever risked.

11
00:00:34,340 --> 00:00:37,760
This is tape market, and this channel is built around one principle.

12
00:00:38,280 --> 00:00:42,900
We look at the numbers, the publicly available data, and the mathematical frameworks the

13
00:00:42,900 --> 00:00:44,620
trading industry already runs on.

14
00:00:45,180 --> 00:00:48,420
No opinions, no hype, just the calculation.

15
00:00:49,540 --> 00:00:54,400
Let's start with the core equation every trader needs to understand, the expectancy formula.

16
00:00:55,100 --> 00:01:02,060
Expectancy equals win rate multiplied by average win minus loss rate multiplied by average loss.

17
00:01:02,620 --> 00:01:03,400
That's it.

18
00:01:03,920 --> 00:01:10,960
That one formula tells you on average how much you can expect to make or lose per trade over a large number of trades.

19
00:01:11,360 --> 00:01:15,160
If expectancy is positive, the strategy has a mathematical edge.

20
00:01:15,680 --> 00:01:20,320
If it's negative or zero, no amount of good trading psychology will save it.

21
00:01:20,320 --> 00:01:23,720
You are mathematically guaranteed to lose money over time.

22
00:01:24,420 --> 00:01:25,720
Let's put in real numbers.

23
00:01:26,480 --> 00:01:28,520
Say a strategy has a 40% win rate.

24
00:01:28,980 --> 00:01:30,660
That means 60% of trades lose.

25
00:01:31,520 --> 00:01:36,040
Now assume every winning trade makes $200 and every losing trade loses $100.

26
00:01:36,680 --> 00:01:41,560
That's a one to two risk to reward ratio, risking $1 to make $2.

27
00:01:42,500 --> 00:01:49,400
Expectancy equals 0.40 times 200 minus 0.60 times 100.

28
00:01:49,960 --> 00:01:54,540
That's 80 minus 60, which equals positive $20 per trade on average.

29
00:01:55,320 --> 00:02:00,160
So even though this strategy loses more often than it wins, losing 60% of the time,

30
00:02:00,560 --> 00:02:05,240
it is still profitable because the size of the wins outweighs the frequency of the losses.

31
00:02:05,800 --> 00:02:09,240
This is the first major insight serious traders understand.

32
00:02:09,860 --> 00:02:12,660
Win rate and risk reward are not separate numbers.

33
00:02:13,160 --> 00:02:14,020
They are a system.

34
00:02:14,700 --> 00:02:16,400
One compensates for the other.

35
00:02:17,320 --> 00:02:18,200
Now let's flip it.

36
00:02:18,620 --> 00:02:20,080
Say a trader has a high win rate.

37
00:02:20,540 --> 00:02:22,780
70% feels amazing on paper.

38
00:02:23,300 --> 00:02:24,920
But the risk reward is poor.

39
00:02:25,520 --> 00:02:31,360
They risk $200 to make only $100 because they cut winners early and let losers run.

40
00:02:31,920 --> 00:02:35,180
Which is actually one of the most common patterns seen in retail trading data.

41
00:02:35,820 --> 00:02:40,880
Expectancy equals 0.70 times 100 minus 0.30 times 200.

42
00:02:41,320 --> 00:02:45,280
That's 70 minus 60, which equals positive $10 per trade.

43
00:02:45,920 --> 00:02:49,040
Still technically profitable, but notice something important.

44
00:02:49,600 --> 00:02:55,080
The high win rate strategy has a much thinner margin than the lower win rate strategy we calculated

45
00:02:55,080 --> 00:02:57,180
earlier, $10 versus $20.

46
00:02:58,140 --> 00:03:03,680
This is exactly why win rate by itself is a misleading metric when it's presented in isolation.

47
00:03:03,680 --> 00:03:07,120
Which is exactly how it tends to get presented in most trading content.

48
00:03:07,560 --> 00:03:10,120
Now here's where it becomes genuinely interesting.

49
00:03:10,660 --> 00:03:12,300
The break even win rate formula.

50
00:03:12,900 --> 00:03:19,160
This tells you the minimum win rate required for a given risk reward ratio just to not lose money

51
00:03:19,160 --> 00:03:20,740
before even talking about profit.

52
00:03:21,780 --> 00:03:26,400
Break even win rate equals risk divided by risk plus reward.

53
00:03:26,980 --> 00:03:31,860
Let's calculate a few combinations because this is the part every trader should have memorized.

54
00:03:32,420 --> 00:03:36,720
If the risk reward ratio is 1 to 1, risking $1 to make $1,

55
00:03:37,120 --> 00:03:43,600
break even win rate equals 1 divided by 1 plus 1, which equals 1 divided by 2, that's 50%.

56
00:03:43,600 --> 00:03:45,100
Makes intuitive sense.

57
00:03:45,540 --> 00:03:49,580
If wins and losses are equal size, you need to win half the time just to break even.

58
00:03:50,560 --> 00:03:57,480
If the risk reward ratio is 1 to 2, risking $1 to make $2, break even win rate equals 1 divided

59
00:03:57,480 --> 00:04:03,340
by 1 plus 2, which equals 1 divided by 3. That's approximately 33.3%.

60
00:04:03,340 --> 00:04:08,740
With this ratio, a trader only needs to win roughly 1 out of every 3 trades to break even.

61
00:04:09,180 --> 00:04:10,640
Anything above that becomes profit.

62
00:04:11,600 --> 00:04:15,840
If the risk reward ratio is 1 to 3, risking $1 to make $3,

63
00:04:16,360 --> 00:04:23,300
break even win rate equals 1 divided by 1 plus 3, which equals 1 divided by 4, that's 25%.

64
00:04:23,300 --> 00:04:28,640
A trader using this ratio can be wrong three out of four times and still not lose money,

65
00:04:28,920 --> 00:04:34,780
mathematically. Now compare that to a 2 to 1 ratio, risking $2 to make $1,

66
00:04:35,060 --> 00:04:39,680
which is common among traders who panic exit winners but hold onto losing positions,

67
00:04:39,960 --> 00:04:40,900
hoping for a recovery.

68
00:04:41,660 --> 00:04:47,340
Break even win rate equals 2 divided by 2 plus 1, which equals 2 divided by 3,

69
00:04:47,340 --> 00:04:54,160
that's approximately 66.7%. That means a trader needs to be right two out of every three times

70
00:04:54,160 --> 00:04:59,180
just to avoid losing money. That is an extremely difficult bar to clear consistently,

71
00:04:59,340 --> 00:05:03,280
based on publicly available long-term performance data across retail accounts.

72
00:05:03,980 --> 00:05:09,420
This single formula explains why so many statistically skilled traders with decent market

73
00:05:09,420 --> 00:05:14,980
reading ability still end up losing. Their risk reward structure demands a win rate that is simply

74
00:05:14,980 --> 00:05:20,960
unrealistic to sustain. Now let's bring in prop firm math specifically, because the calculation

75
00:05:20,960 --> 00:05:27,060
changes once a draw-down limit and profit target enter the picture. Most prop firm evaluations have

76
00:05:27,060 --> 00:05:33,680
three fixed numbers, a profit target, usually around 8 to 10% of the account, a maximum draw-down

77
00:05:33,680 --> 00:05:39,940
limit, usually 10%, meaning if the account drops 10% from its starting balance, the evaluation fails,

78
00:05:40,540 --> 00:05:46,240
and a daily draw-down limit, often around 5%, meaning a single day's loss cannot exceed that.

79
00:05:46,700 --> 00:05:52,360
Let's calculate what risk reward and win rate combination is needed to pass using a 10% profit

80
00:05:52,360 --> 00:05:59,820
target and a 10% max draw-down on a hypothetical $100,000 account. Say a trader risks 0.5% of the

81
00:05:59,820 --> 00:06:06,680
account per trade, that's $500 per trade. To reach the 10% target, which is $10,000 using our

82
00:06:06,680 --> 00:06:12,800
expectancy formula, we need to know how many trades it will mathematically take. If the strategy has

83
00:06:12,800 --> 00:06:22,700
a 1 to 2 risk reward ratio, meaning $500 risked to make a $1000 and a 45% win rate, well above the 33.3%

84
00:06:22,700 --> 00:06:30,720
break even we calculated earlier, expectancy per trade equals 0.45 times a thousand, minus 0.55 times

85
00:06:30,720 --> 00:06:40,140
500, that's 450 minus 275, which equals positive $175 of expected value per trade on average.

86
00:06:40,740 --> 00:06:47,940
To reach $10,000 mathematically, that requires approximately 10,000 divided by 175, which equals roughly

87
00:06:47,940 --> 00:06:54,380
57 trades, on average accounting for variance. Now here's the critical part, the draw-down constraint,

88
00:06:54,800 --> 00:07:02,400
with $500 risked per trade and a 10% max draw-down of 10,000, a trader can withstand 20 consecutive

89
00:07:02,400 --> 00:07:09,580
losing trades before breaching the limit. Given a 45% win rate, meaning up 55% chance of losing any

90
00:07:09,580 --> 00:07:15,180
single trade, the probability of 20 losses in a row is statistically almost negligible,

91
00:07:15,580 --> 00:07:21,560
but strings of 6, 7, or 8 consecutive losses are well within normal statistical variance for that

92
00:07:21,560 --> 00:07:27,100
win rate, and this is exactly the kind of draw-down data every prop firm risk model accounts for.

93
00:07:27,560 --> 00:07:33,560
This is why position sizing is mathematically inseparable from the win rate and risk reward combination.

94
00:07:34,180 --> 00:07:39,640
A trader can have a profitable expectancy and still fail in evaluation, purely because their

95
00:07:39,640 --> 00:07:46,100
position size relative to the draw-down limit was too large, causing a statistically normal losing

96
00:07:46,100 --> 00:07:52,260
streak to breach the account. Let's run one more calculation, the Kelly criterion. The formula

97
00:07:52,260 --> 00:07:58,000
institutional risk desks used to calculate the mathematically optimal percentage of capital to risk

98
00:07:58,000 --> 00:08:05,020
per trade, given a known win rate and risk reward ratio. Kelly percentage equals win rate minus

99
00:08:05,440 --> 00:08:13,380
loss rate divided by risk reward ratio. Using our earlier example, 45% win rate, 1 to 2 risk reward

100
00:08:13,380 --> 00:08:24,560
ratio, Kelly percentage equals 0.45 minus 0.55 divided by 2. That's 0.45 minus 0.275, which equals 0.175,

101
00:08:24,620 --> 00:08:32,960
or 17.5%. Now, this does not mean risking 17.5% of an account per trade. That number represents the

102
00:08:32,960 --> 00:08:39,380
theoretical ceiling before the math starts working against the trader. In practice, professional risk

103
00:08:39,380 --> 00:08:46,480
management data consistently shows that quarter Kelly or even 10th Kelly sizing, so roughly 1.7%

104
00:08:46,480 --> 00:08:53,620
to 4.3% in this case, produces far smoother equity curves with dramatically lower draw-down variants

105
00:08:53,620 --> 00:09:00,600
based on long-run simulation data across thousands of trade sequences. So let's bring this all together

106
00:09:00,600 --> 00:09:07,140
into one clear framework, purely from the mathematics. First, win rate alone means nothing without the

107
00:09:07,140 --> 00:09:13,420
paired risk reward ratio. Second, the break-even win rate formula, risk divided by risk plus reward,

108
00:09:13,700 --> 00:09:18,760
tells you the exact minimum performance of strategy needs before profit even enters the conversation.

109
00:09:19,500 --> 00:09:25,740
Third, expectancy, win rate times average win, minus loss rate times average loss, is the single

110
00:09:25,740 --> 00:09:32,460
number that actually determines long-term profitability. And fourth, for prop firm evaluations specifically,

111
00:09:32,460 --> 00:09:39,000
position sizing relative to the draw-down limit determines survivability, independent of whether the

112
00:09:39,000 --> 00:09:46,020
underlying strategy has positive expectancy. These four calculations together are the actual mathematical

113
00:09:46,020 --> 00:09:51,740
skeleton underneath every profitable trading strategy that exists. Not the entry signal,

114
00:09:52,020 --> 00:09:57,740
not the indicator, not the chart pattern, the math of risk, reward, and position size.

115
00:09:58,360 --> 00:10:03,640
If this kind of data-driven breakdown is useful, the next video on this channel will go deeper into

116
00:10:03,640 --> 00:10:09,620
variance and draw-down probability modeling. The statistics behind exactly how long a losing streak

117
00:10:09,620 --> 00:10:15,640
can realistically run, even with a profitable strategy, and what the data says about it. This has been

118
00:10:15,640 --> 00:10:16,100
tape market.

